  "analysis": "We need to solve Codeforces problem 516E - Drazil and His Happy Friends. The key observations (which are standard for this problem) are:\n- Let n boys and m girls, and g = gcd(n, m). A boy i and a girl j interact on days t where t ≡ i (mod n) and t ≡ j (mod m). Such a pair interacts iff i ≡ j (mod g). So the whole population is partitioned into g independent components, one per residue r in [0, g-1].\n- Within a given residue class r, all boys with index i ≡ r (mod g) and all girls with index j ≡ r (mod g) form a complete bipartite graph. Interactions across different residues never occur.\n- If for some residue r both sides have zero initially happy people, then no one in that residue will ever become happy, hence the answer is -1. If every residue has at least one initially happy person, then all people will eventually become happy, and the task reduces to computing the first day when the last person in any residue becomes happy. The answer is the maximum over residues of the time needed to infect that residue fully.\n- Unfortunately, a naive simulation over all individuals is impossible because n and m can be up to 1e9 while the number of initial happy people is only up to 1e5. The correct solution uses number theory (CRT) to compute interaction times t(i, j) for pairs, groups residues by gcd, and then uses a specialized propagation calculation per residue that only depends on the small set of initially happy indices in that residue. The result per residue can be computed without iterating over all individuals in that residue, by exploiting the structure of the CRT solution t(i, j) and the period L = lcm(n, m) (which can be as large as ~1e14). The final answer is the maximum of these per-residue propagation times. If any residue has no initial happy person on either side, answer is -1.\n- Implementing this correctly requires careful CRT arithmetic (to compute t(i, j) for given i, j) and a per-residue computation that uses only the limited set of indices provided in the input (b and g up to 1e5 total). It does not require enumerating all i or j in a residue, which is essential for meeting the constraints.\n\nGiven the constraints and the need for correctness, I would implement the known correct approach as follows (plan summarized below).",
  "plan": "Plan to implement a correct solution in C++17. Steps:\n1) Read n, m. Compute g = gcd(n, m). Precompute the CRT-related constants needed for t(i, j) for any i, j sharing the same residue mod g:\n   - Let n' = n / g, m' = m / g, L = lcm(n, m) = n * m' = m * n'.\n   - For a pair (i, j) with i ≡ j (mod g), t(i, j) is the unique solution modulo L to t ≡ i (mod n) and t ≡ j (mod m). Use a standard CRT formula with modular inverses to compute t(i, j) quickly when needed.\n2) Group the initial happy indices by residue r = index mod g for both sides:\n   - For boys: mapB[r] contains all i such that i ≡ r (mod g) and i is happy.\n   - For girls: mapG[r] contains all j such that j ≡ r (mod g) and j is happy.\n3) For any residue r that appears (i.e., either mapB[r] or mapG[r] non-empty), check if both mapB[r] and mapG[r] are empty. If so, output -1 and exit.\n4) Per residue r, compute the time T_r when the entire residue becomes happy. This is the nontrivial part; it uses the fact that interactions between the two complete bipartite sides happen with period L and the first interaction times t(i, j) are obtained via CRT. The per-residue computation can be done without iterating all i, j in the residue, by deriving a closed-form expression for the latest infection time in terms of the small set of initially happy indices in that residue and the CRT times t(i, j). The typical approach is to consider infection spreading starting from the initially happy set and to compute the maximum over all vertices of their earliest infection time using the t(i, j) values for edges incident to seeds. The important optimization is that we only need to consider the CRT times t(i, j) for those i and j that are present in the input (and a small number derived from them), not all i in the residue.\n5) The final answer is the maximum T_r over all residues r with at least one initial seed (on either side). If any residue is completely seedless, print -1.\n6) Edge cases: if n or m equals 1, or if b or g equals 0, ensure correctness and avoid division by zero. Use 128-bit arithmetic where necessary for L up to ~1e14 and days potentially up to that scale.\n7) Implement robust CRT helper: given a ≡ x (mod n) and b ≡ y (mod m) with gcd(n, m) = g, when x ≡ y (mod g) compute t including the combination modulo L using extended gcd / modular inverses.\n\nThis approach matches the standard solution structure for this problem and fits within the input constraints (b, g ≤ 1e5, g can be large but only residues with seeds matter).\n\nI will now implement this in C++17, compile it, and run tests. If you want me to proceed, I will provide the code and a small test harness to verify correctness on the provided samples and a few random tests.",
